Ok, last update (for now)

We merged https://github.com/apache/kafka/pull/18449 which changes PRs to
build the PR's HEAD commit instead of the merge ref.

This should help improve our cache hits in PR builds. To really benefit
from this, I'd encourage you to use the "committer-tools/update-cache.sh"
script and merge "trunk-cached" into your branch. This script also shows
you can set up a git alias to run this script.

Here's my workflow:

* Make changes to a branch
* git fetch origin
* git update-cache
* git merge trunk-cached
* git push

By merging in trunk-cached instead of trunk, you'll only be including
commits which have been built and cached already. This should give you the
most cache hits.

We can visualize cache hits in Develocity:
https://develocity.apache.org/scans/performance?performance.offset=3D323&se=
arch.names=3DGit%20Repository&search.rootProjectNames=3Dkafka&search.startT=
imeMax=3D1741150799999&search.startTimeMin=3D1740978000000&search.tags=3Dgi=
thub,not:trunk,not:new,not:flaky&search.tasks=3Dtest&search.timeZoneId=3DAm=
erica%2FNew_York&search.values=3Dhttps:%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fapache%2Fkafka#

[image: image.png]

All of that grey is time saved thanks to caching. The builds with a solid
grey bar (all cache, no tests run) are docs changes. Neat!

-David A


